    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "97686e13-6b7d-3597-80ad-3a8c9e05144d",
                "word": "wester",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3bf35a1c-a2e1-35c8-a33c-5eb930172389",
                        "entry_id": "97686e13-6b7d-3597-80ad-3a8c9e05144d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Western, westerly."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "dialectal",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ac358b8c-ec6f-4a3a-b92e-e5533c0967cb",
                                "sense_id": "3bf35a1c-a2e1-35c8-a33c-5eb930172389",
                                "text": "This is properly two, if not three towns — there being an Easter Anstruther and a Wester Anstruther, both burghs, besides a large fishing village […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fad743f5-cccb-4b34-82ee-33b98d2d5459",
                                "sense_id": "3bf35a1c-a2e1-35c8-a33c-5eb930172389",
                                "text": "There had been a Little and a Meikle, and an Easter and a Wester Coull two centuries ago; and there had been a castle on the property […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e3e008ca-d1f1-49fe-a1cf-2ccc46e8d0a7",
                                "sense_id": "3bf35a1c-a2e1-35c8-a33c-5eb930172389",
                                "text": "It is styled, as we have seen, Wester Rires, which implies an Easter Rires; and this last portion of it probably lay to the north-east, and included […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "914a7f7b-464c-46bb-bb88-94a1b6e362d4",
                                "sense_id": "3bf35a1c-a2e1-35c8-a33c-5eb930172389",
                                "text": "'The fact remains that there is an Easter Fintry and a Wester Fintry in this part of the world. Just as there is an Easter Golford and a Wester Golford, ..."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b111ab6f-ff0e-3adc-a838-4914b17baec0",
                        "entry_id": "97686e13-6b7d-3597-80ad-3a8c9e05144d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "comparative form of west: more west"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "comparative",
                            "form-of",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9540885f-7c3a-4dcd-ac00-46cb86f073e5",
                                "sense_id": "b111ab6f-ff0e-3adc-a838-4914b17baec0",
                                "text": "President-elect Nixon [...] pointed out that Alaska is even wester than Hawaii. [...] had Mr. Nixon searched just a few leagues wester our Secretary of the Interior might have turned out to be a citizen of Kichighinsk. Nor is there anything in the history of the United States that validates the presupposition that anyone who is wester than anybody is simultaneously and necessatily more interior than anybody."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
